INT. NEW DAWN FERTILITY CLINIC - RECEPTION - NIGHT

Empty waiting room. Soft jazz on a dead speaker. Rain streaks the windows.

Orchids. Glossy brochures. A wall of baby photos.

JO, 30s, receptionist, locks the front drawer. Hurries.

Her phone BUZZES.

She snatches it. Reads. Pales.

RAY, 40s, night security, strolls in with a flashlight and a donut.

RAY
Working late, Jo?

JO
Just leaving.

RAY
Door's alarmed. Nobody leaves till eleven.

She backs toward the hall.

JO
Ray. Please.

BEEP-BEEP-BEEP. The alarm panel blinks red.

Ray's smile dies. He drops the donut.

RAY
What did you take?

Down the hall, a FREEZER DOOR SLAMS.

Both turn.

BEA, 30s, lab tech, sprints out of the lab with a steel canister in her arms. Frost pours off it. Her scrubs are soaked.

BEA
Go! Go! Go!

RAY
Put it down!

BEA
Those are eleven families, Ray!

JO
She's right. Look at the logs. Somebody's been selling them.

Ray hesitates. His radio CRACKLES.

RADIO (V.O.)
Unit four, status?

A shadow fills the glass front doors.

VIC, 50s, in a black coat, a clinic director's badge swinging from his neck, raises a keycard. The lock CLICKS.

VIC
Nobody move.

Jo grabs the canister. Bea shoves Ray. The alarm SCREAMS.

They bolt for the fire exit.
